      "text": "We start by examining the multiples of $3$. We are looking for the greatest number that, when cubed and compared to $1000$, does not exceed this value. To find this number, we need to find the largest integer that, when cubed, is still smaller or equal to $1000$. As a multiplier, $9$ can be used to calculate the cube of these multiples and check if the result is less than $1000$.\n\\begin{align*}\nx^3 &= 27, \\\\\n2^3 &= 27 &lt; 3^3 = 65. \\\\\nx^3 &= 343, \\\\\n5^3 &= 125 &lt; 6^3 = 216.\n\\end{align*}\nThen, for higher cubes, the populations can not be \\(>100\\).\nThus, the largest \\(3\\)-multiple of \\(x\\) in which \\(x^3|\\leq1000\\) is \\(3x = 3 * 27 == 81\\).\nThe final, In LaTeX format., The largest positive multiple of \\(3\\) such that its cube is less than \\(1000\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
