      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) where \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of 3 and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we start by determining the maximum integer value for \\( x \\) that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, we estimate the cube root of 1000, which will help us find the upper limit for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10\n\\]\nSo, we have \\( x^3 < 1000 \\). To confirm, we check the cubes of some multiples of 3:\n\\[\n3^3 = 27 \\quad (3^3 < 1000)\n\\]\n\\[\n6^3 = 216 \\quad (6^3 < 1000)\n\\]\n\\[\n9^3 = 729 \\quad (9^3 < 1000)\n\\]\n\\[\n12^3 = 1728\n\\]\nHere, \\( 12^3 = 1728 \\) is a number greater than 1000. Therefore, the greatest multiple of 3 that satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) is 11, since \\( 11^3 = 1331 \\) which is still less than 1000.\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
